Question 11 — Macro / Standardization

How do you use standard macros or reusable programming tools to improve consistency and efficiency across a large oncology TLF package?

**Polished Version** (ready for interview):

---

For a large oncology TLF package, I would leverage standard macros and reusable programming tools to improve consistency, efficiency, and overall quality.

I would first review sponsor-level or therapeutic-area standard macros, as well as reference macros from previous similar oncology studies. Rather than copying them directly, I would compare the previous study requirements against the current SAP, ADaM specifications, table shells, and sponsor standards to determine what can be reused and what requires study-specific adaptation.

Standard macros are particularly useful for treatment group setup, population denominators, summary statistics, TEAE tables, lab shift tables, Kaplan-Meier outputs, titles, footnotes, pagination, and output formatting. Using them helps ensure consistency across programmers and reduces the risk of table-to-table discrepancies.

If a macro requires modification, I would clearly separate global standard logic from local study-specific parameters. I would document the macro version, input assumptions, key parameters, and any study-specific changes. Before rolling it out across the package, I would validate the macro outputs against manual checks or independent programming.

For a large TLF package, I would also ensure all programmers use the same macro library, formats, templates, and setup programs. If a macro issue is identified, I would assess all affected outputs, fix the root cause centrally, rerun the impacted outputs, and document the resolution in the QC log.

The goal is not only efficiency, but also consistency, traceability, and audit readiness across the entire submission package.

